collection records, or institutional pages). Do not include any URLs from 
en.wikipedia.org and do not cite Wikipedia or obvious Wikipedia mirrors.\n6. 
Reflect the main scholarly positions and disagreements captured in the graph: 
Evans’s original presentation of the Ring of Nestor as a Late Minoan gold signet
ring from a tomb at Thisbe in Boeotia; descriptions of its material, dimensions 
(if available), and figural imagery; later scholarship that questions the ring’s
authenticity or assigns it to a later, possibly modern, pseudo-Minoan or hybrid 
tradition influenced by Egyptian New Kingdom iconography; and discussions of its
role in the study of Minoan and Aegean religion and in the historiography of 
collecting and forgery.\n7. Where scholarly opinions differ (for example, on 
