submit_final_report({'title': 'Ring of Nestor', 'sections': [{'title': 'Ring of 
Nestor', 'section_type': 'lead', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor is a 
large gold signet ring associated with Aegean archaeology and long discussed in 
relation to Minoan religion and ideas about the afterlife.[1][2][3] Originally 
presented by Arthur Evans as part of a group of gold signet rings and bead seals
reportedly found in a funerary context at Thisbe in Boeotia, Greece, the ring 
was classified by him as a Late Minoan work of the Late Bronze Age.[2] Its bezel
carries a complex engraved figural scene that Evans interpreted as a vision of 
the "Minoan after-world", and the ring subsequently became a widely cited 
example in studies of Minoan iconography and eschatology.[1][2][3] More recent 
scholarship, however, refers to the object as the "Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring" 
and argues that its imagery and problematic provenance point to a modern or 
hybrid creation strongly influenced by Egyptian New Kingdom funerary 
iconography, leading to ongoing debate about its authenticity, date, and 
cultural attribution.[1][3]', 'source_ids': ['20260628172643164788_37c45901', 
'20260628172643247546_74c92b41', '20260628172643306973_c6bca36b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}], 'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628172645928296_427ccd7b', 
'20260628172646088018_ea0219c9']}, {'title': 'Discovery and provenance', 
'section_type': 'section', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Arthur Evans introduced the Ring
of Nestor in a study titled "The ring of Nestor: A glimpse into the Minoan 
after-world and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia".[2] In this account he described the ring as belonging to a 
group of gold signet rings and bead seals said to come from a tomb at Thisbe in 
Boeotia, which he characterised as a "sepulchral treasure".[2] On this basis the
ring entered scholarly literature as a gold signet ring from a Greek mainland 
funerary context, associated with burial practices and elite grave 
goods.[1][2]', 'source_ids': ['20260628172643247546_74c92b41', 
'20260628172643164788_37c45901'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later 
re‑examinations of the object’s history have questioned the reliability of the 
reported findspot and excavation circumstances at Thisbe.[3] A detailed study of
the "Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring" reviews the archaeological background and chain 
of ownership, arguing that inconsistencies in the Thisbe narrative and the 
ring’s circulation on the art market complicate the attribution of a secure tomb
context.[3] This work emphasises that the collection history and market 
trajectory of the ring are crucial for assessing its authenticity and for 
understanding how it came to be integrated into discussions of Aegean 
prehistory.[3]', 'source_ids': ['20260628172643306973_c6bca36b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}], 'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628172646088018_ea0219c9']}, 
{'title': 'Description', 'section_type': 'section', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The 
Ring of Nestor is consistently described as a large, heavy gold signet ring, 
with a substantial bezel bearing an engraved figural composition.[1][2] Evans 
stressed the ring’s size and thickness and presented the engraved scene as a 
finely executed relief, comparable in craftsmanship to Late Minoan gold rings 
from Crete.[2] Subsequent iconographic studies similarly refer to it as a gold 
signet ring of notable dimensions and elaborate workmanship, situating it 
within, or in conscious imitation of, the corpus of Minoan and pseudo‑Minoan 
seal imagery.[1][3]', 'source_ids': ['20260628172643247546_74c92b41', 
'20260628172643164788_37c45901', '20260628172643306973_c6bca36b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'A signet ring, in this context, is a finger ring whose 
bezel carries an engraved design intended to be impressed into clay or other 
soft material as a seal; in Bronze Age Aegean contexts such rings are often 
associated with administrative or ritual functions.[1][2] Evans understood the 
Ring of Nestor within this broader category of sealing devices and compared its 
engraved imagery and technical execution to those of other Aegean gold signet 
rings.[2]', 'source_ids': ['20260628172643247546_74c92b41', 
'20260628172643164788_37c45901'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}], 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': []}, {'title': 'Iconography and interpretation', 
'section_type': 'section', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The bezel of the Ring of Nestor 
carries a complex figural scene that Evans famously interpreted as a glimpse of 
the "Minoan after-world".[2] His description emphasised the presence of human 
figures, a sacred architectural structure, and various symbols that he 
associated with the realm of the dead or the underworld, construing the 
composition as a religious vision related to beliefs about the afterlife.[2] 
This reading contributed to the ring’s prominence in discussions of Minoan 
religion and eschatology, where it was often cited as visual evidence for 
conceptions of an afterlife in Minoan culture.[1][2][3]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628172643247546_74c92b41', '20260628172643164788_37c45901', 
